Astrometry.net (plate solving)
  https://astrometry.net
  Copyright (c) 2006-2015, Astrometry.net Developers.

  Astrometry.net's own code is licensed under a 3-clause BSD license.
  Because it uses libraries licensed under the GNU GPL, Astrometry.net as
  a whole is distributed under the GNU General Public License, version 3
  or later.

  Astrosolve runs Astrometry.net's "solve-field" program as a separate
  program. It is not compiled into or linked with DB Astro Suite's code.
  The Astrosolve Docker image installs the unmodified Debian package
  "astrometry.net". Its license and copyright notice are included in the
  image at /usr/share/doc/astrometry.net/copyright, and its complete
  source code is available from Debian:

    https://sources.debian.org/src/astrometry.net/
    https://snapshot.debian.org/package/astrometry.net/ (every version)

  You can also request that source code by opening an issue at
  https://github.com/sidthesloth92/db-astro-suite/issues.

  If you use Astrometry.net results in published research, the
  Astrometry.net team asks that you cite:
    Lang, D., Hogg, D. W., Mierle, K., Blanton, M., & Roweis, S. (2010).
    "Astrometry.net: Blind astrometric calibration of arbitrary
    astronomical images." The Astronomical Journal, 139, 1782.

Astrometry.net index files
  Downloaded from http://data.astrometry.net when the Astrosolve server is
  set up (4100 and 4200 series). They are not included in this repository
  or in the Docker image. They are built from:
    - the Tycho-2 catalogue (ESA Hipparcos mission), and
    - the Two Micron All Sky Survey (2MASS), a joint project of the
      University of Massachusetts and the Infrared Processing and Analysis
      Center/California Institute of Technology, funded by NASA and the
      National Science Foundation.

Object catalogs used for star map labels
  Downloaded when the Astrosolve server is set up. They are not included
  in this repository or in the Docker image.

  - OpenNGC by Mattia Verga, https://github.com/mattiaverga/OpenNGC
    Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0
    International (CC BY-SA 4.0).

  - Gaia DR3 (star positions, Gaia labels and star distances), downloaded
    from the ESA Gaia archive. This work has made use of data from the
    European Space Agency (ESA) mission Gaia
    (https://www.cosmos.esa.int/gaia), processed by the Gaia Data
    Processing and Analysis Consortium (DPAC). Credit: ESA/Gaia/DPAC.
    ESA states that Gaia data are distributed under the CC BY-NC 3.0 IGO
    license (https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/gaia-users/license). That
    license is non-commercial: anyone running a commercial copy of
    Astrosolve must check ESA's terms for Gaia data first.

  - IAU Catalog of Star Names (IAU Working Group on Star Names),
    https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~emamajek/WGSN/

  - Catalogs retrieved through the VizieR catalogue access tool, operated
    at CDS, Strasbourg, France (https://cds.unistra.fr): Hipparcos (I/239),
    Tycho-2 (I/259), Tycho-2 HD identifications (III/231), Sharpless HII
    regions (VII/20), Abell galaxy clusters (VII/110A), RC3 (VII/155),
    HyperLEDA PGC (VII/237), Milliquas (VII/294), Lynds bright nebulae
    (VII/9B), Lynds dark nebulae (VII/7A), Barnard dark nebulae
    (VII/220A), Milky Way Star Clusters (J/A+A/558/A53) and Harris
    globular clusters (VII/202). We acknowledge the usage of the
    HyperLEDA database (http://leda.univ-lyon1.fr).

  - The SIMBAD database, operated at CDS, Strasbourg, France. It supplies
    object distances every time the catalog is built, and the Astrosolve
    server can also query it while solving (off by default).

  - A few rounded distances for famous nebulae, taken from published
    references including the NASA/IPAC Extragalactic Database (NED).

  Astrogram credits all of these on screen: a short line under the star
  map opens a "Data credits" dialog listing every source.

Wikipedia (Astrogram "Wiki" button)
  The Wiki button fetches article summaries from Wikipedia
  (https://en.wikipedia.org). Wikipedia text is licensed under the
  Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 license (CC BY-SA 4.0).
  Astrogram reminds users to credit Wikipedia if they post that text.

